# Vulnerability details

The `phiSignerAddress` in the contract is used to verify that signatures received from the backend are valid. The signatures are checked against this address in the `_validateArtCreationSignature()` function. However, if the `phiSignerAddress` is updated using the `setPhiSignerAddress()` function, all previously issued signatures that are still pending will become invalid. This is because the signer address stored in the smart contract is no longer the same as the address that generated the signatures. As a result, all pending art creation transactions that rely on these signatures will fail.


## Impact
When the `phiSignerAddress` is changed, it causes all pending signatures to become invalid. This will lead to a failure in the art creation process for any artists who have paid for and obtained signatures from the previous address but have not yet completed their transactions. This could result in financial losses, frustration for users, and a disruption in the functionality of the contract, as all pending art creation operations would be halted.

## Proof of Concept
Add this code to the `PhiFactory.t.sol` file and run the following command:
`forge test -vvv --match-test POC`:

```
    function test_POC() public {
        // This creation will pass without any problem
        _createArt(ART_ID_URL_STRING);

        // Owner decided to change the phiSignerAddress
        vm.prank(owner);
        phiFactory.setPhiSignerAddress(address(1337));

        // This creation will revert
        vm.expectRevert(IPhiFactory.AddressNotSigned.selector);
        _createArt(ART_ID2_URL_STRING);
    }
```

## Tools Used
Manual Review 
Forge

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To prevent this issue, it is recommended to carefully manage when and how the `phiSignerAddress` is updated. One approach could be to include a grace period or notification mechanism before the update takes effect, allowing all pending transactions to complete. Another approach could be to store and validate signatures with their respective signer addresses, ensuring that previous signatures remain valid even after the signer address is updated. This way, the contract can handle multiple valid signer addresses over time.











## Assessed type

DoS

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/art/PhiNFT1155.sol#L151


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Severity: High

Likelihood: High

Summary: A malicious user can exploit the underflow vulnerability in the `createArtFromFactory()` function to steal funds from the protocol. The exploit is highly probable since it can be carried out at any time by an attacker.

## Vulnerability details


The `createArtFromFactory()` function in the `PhiNFT1155.sol` contract lacks proper checks to prevent an underflow when calculating the difference between `msg.value` and `artFee`. Specifically, if `msg.value` is less than `artFee`, the subtraction will underflow which will bypassing the check, resulting in an unintended large amount being transferred to the `msg.sender`.

This occurs in the following section of the code:

Link: https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/art/PhiNFT1155.sol#L151

```solidity
function createArtFromFactory(uint256 artId_) external payable onlyPhiFactory whenNotPaused returns (uint256) {
        _artIdToTokenId[artId_] = tokenIdCounter; 
        _tokenIdToArtId[tokenIdCounter] = artId_;

        uint256 artFee = phiFactoryContract.artCreateFee();

        console.log("artFee:", artFee);

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(artFee); 
        emit ArtCreated(artId_, tokenIdCounter); 
        uint256 createdTokenId = tokenIdCounter;

        unchecked { 
            tokenIdCounter += 1; 
        } 

    -->>    if ((msg.value - artFee) > 0) { //@audit potential underflow
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(msg.value - artFee);
        }

        return createdTokenId; 
    } 

```

## Proof of Concept
An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by calling the createArtFromFactory() function with a msg.value less than artFee. This will cause the subtraction to underflow, and a large amount of ETH (due to the underflow wrapping around) will be transferred to the attacker’s address.

## Tools Used
Manual Review, VsCode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To prevent this underflow, the condition should be modified to check if `msg.value` is greater than `artFee` before performing the subtraction and transfer. The updated code would look like this:

```solidity
function createArtFromFactory(uint256 artId_) external payable onlyPhiFactory whenNotPaused returns (uint256) {
        _artIdToTokenId[artId_] = tokenIdCounter; 
        _tokenIdToArtId[tokenIdCounter] = artId_;

        uint256 artFee = phiFactoryContract.artCreateFee();

        console.log("artFee:", artFee);

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(artFee); 
        emit ArtCreated(artId_, tokenIdCounter); 
        uint256 createdTokenId = tokenIdCounter;

        unchecked { 
            tokenIdCounter += 1; 
        } 

        -    if ((msg.value - artFee) > 0) {
        +    if (msg.value > artFee) { 
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(msg.value - artFee);
        }

        return createdTokenId; 
    } 

```

By implementing this change, the underflow issue will be resolved, ensuring that only the correct amount of ETH is transferred back to the sender.




